Support driving the SPI chip-select from a GPIO in the rockchip SPI driver, and
skip the 16-bit flash read optimisation on such buses.

The driver only drove the native chip-select via the SER register. On some
boards the rk3399 native CS is claimed by hardware and can't be released, so
the device must be selected from a GPIO (as Linux supports via cs-gpios). Add
optional cs-gpios support (active-low, per the SPI CS convention), guarded by
CONFIG_IS_ENABLED(DM_GPIO) so SPL (which may build without DM_GPIO) links.

The read path uses a 16-bit "flash reader" that flips the frame size and
toggles the controller enable mid-transfer. That glitches devices which hold CS
across several spi_xfer() calls (e.g. a TPM's TIS protocol). Skip it on buses
that use a GPIO chip-select (not the boot SPI-NOR), falling back to the plain
8-bit path.

